          "type": "turn_end",
          "turn": {
            "text": "Alberto builds agents with an emphasis on measured quality, human oversight and cost control. Here's how:\n\n**Practices**\n- **Offline evals before shipping:** Eval cases are recorded against a real model and replayed in CI with no model or network. A stale recording or an off-topic answer fails the build. ([ADR 0001](https://github.com/acarbonellb/acb-landing-public/blob/main/docs/adr/0001-agent-evals-record-replay.md))\n- **Graded autonomy, human in the loop:** Agents recommend and a human decides. The fraud agent auto-approves only on evidence verified in code.\n- **Cost and limits tracked:** Per-request token budgets, per-IP rate limits and a monthly spend cap. Eval reports show mean tokens and latency per case. ([guardrails](https://github.com/acarbonellb/acb-landing-public#guardrails), [eval report](https://github.com/acarbonellb/acb-landing-public/blob/main/agent/evals/report.md))\n- **Changes measured run over run:** Each eval run is appended to a history, so changes to prompts, tools or content show up against the previous run. ([eval history](https://github.com/acarbonellb/acb-landing-public/blob/main/agent/evals/history.jsonl))\n\n**Example: Fraud Detection Agent** (offline evals, TypeScript, PostgreSQL, GCP)\n- On 31 eval cases, approved fraud fell from 54% to 8% across twelve iterations with a local 9B model.\n- For deployment, Claude Haiku 5.5 scored 86% accuracy vs 71% for the local model, approving 1% of fraud vs 8%, at about $0.002 per review.\n\nOther projects include the Organizer Support Agent (coming soon, using tool calling and MCP) and this portfolio agent (TypeScript, Claude, MCP).",
            "toolCalls": [],
            "stopReason": "end_turn",
            "usage": {
              "inputTokens": 2725,
              "outputTokens": 629
            },
            "providerContent": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Alberto builds agents with an emphasis on measured quality, human oversight and cost control. Here's how:\n\n**Practices**\n- **Offline evals before shipping:** Eval cases are recorded against a real model and replayed in CI with no model or network. A stale recording or an off-topic answer fails the build. ([ADR 0001](https://github.com/acarbonellb/acb-landing-public/blob/main/docs/adr/0001-agent-evals-record-replay.md))\n- **Graded autonomy, human in the loop:** Agents recommend and a human decides. The fraud agent auto-approves only on evidence verified in code.\n- **Cost and limits tracked:** Per-request token budgets, per-IP rate limits and a monthly spend cap. Eval reports show mean tokens and latency per case. ([guardrails](https://github.com/acarbonellb/acb-landing-public#guardrails), [eval report](https://github.com/acarbonellb/acb-landing-public/blob/main/agent/evals/report.md))\n- **Changes measured run over run:** Each eval run is appended to a history, so changes to prompts, tools or content show up against the previous run. ([eval history](https://github.com/acarbonellb/acb-landing-public/blob/main/agent/evals/history.jsonl))\n\n**Example: Fraud Detection Agent** (offline evals, TypeScript, PostgreSQL, GCP)\n- On 31 eval cases, approved fraud fell from 54% to 8% across twelve iterations with a local 9B model.\n- For deployment, Claude Haiku 5.5 scored 86% accuracy vs 71% for the local model, approving 1% of fraud vs 8%, at about $0.002 per review.\n\nOther projects include the Organizer Support Agent (coming soon, using tool calling and MCP) and this portfolio agent (TypeScript, Claude, MCP)."
              }
            ]
